\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Chirality of the torus from vertex degrees}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{On an oriented two-dimensional \vocabk{simplicial momentum grid}{fdip-013X} take the symbols and nondegenerate zeros of the \vocabk{bulk integer}{fdip-019W}. Assume the interpolated triple #{(f_1,f_2,m)} never vanishes and that every point with interpolated #{f=0,m<0} is a vertex. On each consistently oriented triangle #{t} let #{\phi_t} be its angle for one two-dimensional Clifford copy with #{\Gamma=C}. Put #{s=\trace(C\Gamma)/2} for the full module. Its \vocabk{chirality}{fdip-015O} is defined if #{s\phi_t} avoids odd multiples of #{\pi} for every triangle. In that case, with #{k_t} the unique integer for which #{s\phi_t-2\pi k_t\in(-\pi,\pi)},
##{\operatorname{chir}=-s\kappa-\sum_t k_t.}
In particular #{|s\phi_t|<\pi} for all #{t} gives #{\operatorname{chir}=-s\kappa}. The vertex hypotheses hold for the free Wilson symbol with even periods at least #{4} and #{aM\notin\{0,2,4\}}.}

\proof{\p{On one copy choose #{\varepsilon_1=\sigma_1}, #{\varepsilon_2=\sigma_2}, so #{C=-\sigma_3}. In the ordered Pauli coordinates of \ref{fdip-015R} the vector of #{H} is #{(-f_2,f_1,-m)}. Its northern ray meets the interpolant only at the designated zeros. A nearby generic ray counts their local degrees. Indeed choose disjoint small stars around them; the normalized map on the compact complement avoids the north pole and hence a neighborhood of it. On any incident simplex write the rotated centered coefficient as #{J\lambda} in edge coordinates based at a zero, and #{-m=c+d\cdot\lambda}, #{c>0}. Nondegeneracy makes #{J} invertible. For a generic nearby ray #{(t u,1)} with #{t>0} small, its intersection solves
##{\lambda=\frac{tc}{1-t d\cdot J^{-1}u}J^{-1}u.}
The denominator is positive for every incident simplex after one sufficiently small choice of #{t}. The chosen cones and their orientation signs are those of the local degree: the rotation #{(f_1,f_2)\mapsto(-f_2,f_1)} has positive determinant and small perturbations of the invertible linear systems preserve the determinant signs. The ray count is #{\kappa}. The single-line chirality is its negative by \ref{fdip-015R}.}

\p{For a reversed Clifford copy the negative line is the complementary Pauli line. Negating all three normalized directions conjugates the triple projector trace in \ref{fdip-015R}. The overlap determinants multiply in the orthogonal decomposition of \ref{fdip-01A1}, so the argument on #{t} is the principal value of #{s\phi_t}. Summing yields the formula. For Wilson, \ref{fdip-0142} locates every interpolated sine zero at a corner on the given even grid; its mass is nonzero by \ref{fdip-019Y}.}}
